r -->\r\n381:             <div class=\"activity-indicator\" id=\"activity-indicator\">\r\n382:                 <div class=\"listening-state\">\r\n383:                     <div class=\"pulse\"></div>\r\n384:                     <span>Listening...</span>\r\n385:                 </div>\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":2348,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-27T05:26:43Z","thinking":"Initial investigation reveals two distinct views in the web interface: onboarding and live. Observing a similar application's design, a global floating top bar containing essential controls is present. Considering these observations, a persistent top bar or duplicated \"move\" button integration across both views could provide a better user experience.\n\n\nThe repositioning overlay utilizes a 3x2 grid across the screen to define six docking slots for the window. These slots are highlighted upon hover and activate window docking upon click. Examination of an Electron-based application reveals how window boundaries are defined, which raises questions about how the overlay manages positioning within a full-screen context.\n\n\nThe UI card is absolutely positioned within the window, and its location is determined by the selected docking slot. To handle clicks on the transparent areas, mouse events need to be managed based on whether the repositioning overlay is active. When the overlay is open, mouse events are not ignored, allowing clicks through to the underlying slots. Upon slot selection, the card smoothly transitions to the new position, and the overlay is hidden, while also re-enabling mouse event interception.\n\n\nTwo distinct approaches are being considered: resizing the Electron window dynamically versus maintaining a full-screen transparent window and moving the HTML card. Examining a similar application reveals the latter approach is used to avoid Windows-specific issues with resizing transparent windows. Flickering, DWM capture re-computation, focus stealing, and re-rasterization lag are prevented by keeping the window full-screen.\n\n\nThe card's movement between slots will use CSS animations for smooth transitions. An overlay will appear over the entire screen for repositioning.  To allow clicks through to underlying windows when full-screen, the application uses a function to ignore mouse events.  HTML elements still receive mouse events even when the function is active. Therefore, event listeners can be implemented in the renderer to control when mouse events are ignored.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"c:\\\\Users\\\\Arthur Henrique\\\\Documents\\\\GitHub\\\\Spectra\\\\web\\\\css\\\\main.css\"","EndLine":"60","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Viewing web/css/main.css layout rules\"","toolSummary":"\"View main.css layout\""}}]}
