st solution by leaving the main window untouched and displaying the slots as an overlay. This approach simplifies implementation and minimizes risk. Dynamic coordinate calculations for the\n<truncated 705 bytes>\n existing maximum container widths in the CSS should be checked.\n\nA window size of 820x880 would fit comfortably, but the precise placement of the main window's six available positions necessitates calculations using a function that accounts for the available work area, padding, and window dimensions. The existing configuration values will be used.\n\n\nThe user's explicit instruction confirms that window movement is *exclusively* via the six slots; no other dragging will be permitted. Window placement persistence will utilize `localStorage` and Electron's main process for saved location retrieval on boot. A new overlay window, specifically the `moveOverlayWindow`, must be created or shown when the user triggers the movement functionality.\n\n\nThe overlay window will load a dedicated HTML page served by the local Express server. This `move-overlay.html` will define the visual layout as a grid covering the entire screen. The grid contains six slots, each representing a placement position for the main window, styled with rounded rectangles.\n\n\nThe overlay's slot click action triggers an API call to select the chosen location, closes the overlay, sets the main window's bounds to the selected slot's coordinates, and brings the main window to focus. Pressing Escape or clicking outside the overlay closes it without altering window position.\n\nThe main window can be configured to remain visible behind the overlay using a specific setting. This ensures the user sees the screen dimmed with the glowing slots.\n\nThe location of the \"move\" button within the main application interface is under review, focusing on placement within the existing `.brand-header` or a dedicated utility bar.\n\n\nA \"Move\" button will be added to the UI header, triggering the overlay. The existing header will also receive a \"move\" button, along with an indicator for the six-slot grid functionality.\n\nThe HTML for these buttons and their related icons has been defined. The goal is to provide a visual cue to the user regarding the window repositioning feature.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Windows.Forms\\n$screen = [System.Windows.Forms.Screen]::PrimaryScreen\\nWrite-Host \\\"Resolution:\\\" $screen.Bounds.Width \\\"x\\\" $screen.Bounds.Height\\nWrite-Host \\\"Working Area:\\\" $screen.WorkingArea.Width \\\"x\\\" $screen.WorkingArea.Height\"","Cwd":"\"c:\\\\Users\\\\Arthur Henrique\\\\Documents\\\\GitHub\\\\Spectra\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Checking primary screen resolution\"","toolSummary":"\"Get screen resolution\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking"]}
